#: ../../repository.rst:1
msgid "Guidedog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:4
msgid "**Write once. Explain well. Publish on the web and on paper.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:6
msgid "Guidedog builds HTML documentation sites and PDF books from reStructuredText and Markdown. It is written in Rust. Its conversion engine is **Guidedoc**, and the embedded Typst compiler typesets the PDF pages. No Python environment or LaTeX installation is needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:10
msgid "Sphinx inspires the authoring workflow. Guidedog is an independent system: configuration is declarative TOML, API extraction is static, and Python configuration and extensions are never executed. Complete Sphinx compatibility is not a goal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:14
msgid "This page is the repository overview. On GitHub, the ``README`` files at the root of the repository give the same overview in each language of the manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:17
msgid "Start a project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:27
msgid "The project contains ``conf.toml``, ``index.rst``, ``_templates/layout.html``, ``_templates/book.typ``, and ``_static/``. Templates are ordinary files we can edit. HTML provides navigation, search, code highlighting, diagrams, and equations. PDF books provide chapters, cross-references, figures, indexes, and language-aware typesetting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:32
msgid "The default theme is light. Readers can choose dark or the system theme. The manual detects the browser language on its landing page and repository overview. A saved choice takes precedence. Direct links to an edition or chapter remain explicit requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:42
msgid "Install"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:45
msgid "Each `release <https://github.com/insanai/guidedog/releases>`__ has an archive for Linux, macOS, and Windows, with ``SHA256SUMS``. The Typst compiler and the tree-sitter parsers are compiled into ``guidedog``. Diagrams use the Graphviz library (``libgvc``). Where the system does not provide it, the archive's notes say how to install it. Fetching remote intersphinx inventories and publishing to GitHub Pages use the HTTP client built into ``guidedog``, so no ``curl`` program is needed. Check and install the archive:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:58
msgid "The release notes describe each platform's requirements and limits. See the :doc:`installation guide <howto/install>` for the details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:61
msgid "Build from source"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:64
msgid "Install a Rust toolchain (the workspace's ``rust-version`` or later) and the Graphviz development files (``libgvc``, ``libcgraph``; for example ``graphviz-dev`` on Debian and Ubuntu, or ``brew install graphviz``). Then, at the checkout root:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:75
msgid "The ``typst`` crates are a default feature of ``gd-typst``. Without them, Guidedog runs an installed, pinned ``typst`` program for PDF output. `RELEASING.md <https://github.com/insanai/guidedog/blob/main/RELEASING.md>`__ describes the release workflow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:80
msgid "The workspace is split by responsibility. The engine crates (``guidedoc``, the readers and renderers, ``guidedoc-defaults``) use only the standard library. Supporting libraries (``gd-toml``, ``gd-json``, ``gd-jinja``, ``gd-highlight``, ``gd-gettext``, ``gd-graphdog``, ``gd-typst``, ...) never depend on the engine. The project builder and the command line sit above both. The programs are under ``cmd/``. See :doc:`Internals <internals/index>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:86
msgid "Translate with Guidedog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:99
msgid "Translate and review ``msgstr`` values in the PO files. Guidedog extracts POT files, merges catalogs, reports progress, and builds each edition. It does not invent translations. Commands, paths, code, API names, and reference targets stay unchanged. Only the manual and repository overview are translated. API and GDS remain English. See the :doc:`manual build instructions <howto/maintain-docs>` for assembling all editions and PDF books."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:105
msgid "Extend and publish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:108
msgid "Write a build plugin or a publisher with the :doc:`step-by-step plugin chapter <plugins>`. A plugin implements one Rust trait. The same value is linked into a custom ``guidedog`` binary with ``gd_cli::run_with``, or served as an installed program with ``gd_plugins::serve_plugin``. The chapter then follows the real GitHub Pages publisher through ownership, credentials, conditional updates and recovery. ``guidedog publish --timeout=SECONDS`` bounds each complete exchange (default: 300 seconds). We choose a value longer than ``--wait``. After an unknown outcome, we recover before publishing again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:117
msgid "Keep discussions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:120
msgid "``guidedog dsc`` keeps proposals and decisions as reStructuredText records, one file each. It builds them into a website and PDF books. Each catalog has a prefix: Guidedog's design records use ``gds``, and another project might use ``eds``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../repository.rst:133
msgid "A catalog's ``discussion.toml`` sets its lifecycle states, record types, and numbering. ``guidedog dsc config`` prints it. The commands come from the discussion plugin, which every ``guidedog`` links, so nothing else needs to be installed. Guidedog's own ``docs/gds`` is one such catalog: we run ``guidedog dsc check`` in that folder. See :doc:`Keep a discussion catalog <howto/discussions>`."
msgstr ""

